What this licence covers, and what it does not

The MIT licence above covers this repository's own files: the integration
under waterbox/, the test game generator and its tune under waterbox/tests/,
the documents and the build scripts (setup-mesa.sh is the touchHLE core's,
under the same terms).

It does NOT cover the engine. The engine is the RSDKv4 decompilation
(github.com/RSDKModding/RSDKv4-Decompilation, the submodule extern/RSDKv4), a
decompilation of Christian Whitehead's Retro Engine v4, under its own terms,
the "RSDKv3/v4 DECOMPILATION SOURCE CODE LICENSE v2", whose text is in
LICENSE-RSDK.txt and in every package. The files in patches/ change the
decompilation's source, so they are modified copies of it and are under those
terms too. In short, those terms allow copying, changing and distributing
for public or private use as long as:

  - any build given to anyone has the downloadable content off by default
    (this core is built with RSDK_AUTOBUILD, which forces it off);
  - nothing is used for profit;
  - the decompilation and the original are credited to their authors, and
    not claimed as one's own (original Retro Engine author: Christian
    "Taxman" Whitehead; decompilation authors: Rubberduckycooly and st×tic);
  - no game asset is distributed (this package carries none: the game's
    data file is the user's);
  - the licence travels with every modified copy of the source.

A built package therefore combines this repository's code (MIT), the
decompilation (its licence above) with the stb_image it carries (MIT or
public domain), SDL 2 (zlib licence), libogg and libvorbis (BSD-3-Clause),
and Mesa (MIT); its effective terms are the decompilation's, and it may not
be used commercially. waterbox/package-licenses.json lists every part, and
every package carries their texts.
